## 4. Network Load Balancer (NLB)

### NLB Overview

NLB works at Layer 4 (Transport Layer):
- Extreme performance (millions of requests/sec)
- Ultra-low latency (~100 microseconds)
- Handles TCP/UDP traffic
- Perfect for: Gaming, real-time apps, IoT, DNS, VoIP

### Create NLB

```bash
# Create Network Load Balancer
NLB=$(aws elbv2 create-load-balancer \
  --name my-nlb \
  --subnets subnet-public-1a subnet-public-1b \
  --scheme internet-facing \
  --type network \
  --ip-address-type ipv4 \
  --query 'LoadBalancers[0].LoadBalancerArn' \
  --output text)

# Create UDP Target Group for gaming
NLB_TG=$(aws elbv2 create-target-group \
  --name gaming-servers \
  --protocol UDP \
  --port 9000 \
  --vpc-id vpc-12345678 \
  --target-type instance \
  --health-check-protocol TCP \
  --health-check-port 9000 \
  --query 'TargetGroups[0].TargetGroupArn' \
  --output text)

# Create UDP Listener
aws elbv2 create-listener \
  --load-balancer-arn $NLB \
  --protocol UDP \
  --port 9000 \
  --default-actions Type=forward,TargetGroupArn=$NLB_TG

# Register gaming servers
aws elbv2 register-targets \
  --target-group-arn $NLB_TG \
  --targets Id=i-gaming-1 Id=i-gaming-2 Id=i-gaming-3
```

### NLB Features

- **Connection Draining** - Gracefully close connections before removing instance
- **Preserve Source IP** - Important for real-time apps
- **Flow Hash Algorithm** - Same flow always goes to same target
- **Cross-Zone Load Balancing** - Distribute across AZs
- **TLS Termination** - NLB can terminate TLS for extreme performance

### NLB Use Cases

- **Gaming**: Millions of players, UDP protocol, ultra-low latency
- **IoT Devices**: Millions of sensors, high throughput data ingestion
- **Real-time Communication**: Video streaming, VoIP, multiplayer apps
- **DNS Services**: DNS queries, extreme throughput needed
- **Financial Trading**: Microsecond-level latency critical

