## 12. Best Practices

### Load Balancer Design

✓ DO:
- Use ALB for web/microservices (default choice)
- Use NLB only for extreme performance needs
- Multi-AZ always for HA
- Enable access logs for troubleshooting
- Use security groups (restrict traffic)
- Enable HTTPS/TLS everywhere
- Monitor with CloudWatch
- Auto-scale based on metrics

✗ DON'T:
- Use single availability zone
- Leave HTTP unencrypted
- Use CLB for new applications
- Forget to configure health checks
- Leave default security groups
- Ignore access logs
- Over-provision capacity

### Security Best Practices

```bash
# Restrict inbound traffic
aws ec2 authorize-security-group-ingress \
  --group-id $SG \
  --protocol tcp \
  --port 443 \
  --cidr 0.0.0.0/0

# Target security group (only from LB)
aws ec2 authorize-security-group-ingress \
  --group-id $TARGET_SG \
  --protocol tcp \
  --port 8080 \
  --source-group $LB_SG

# Use latest TLS policies
aws elbv2 modify-listener \
  --listener-arn $LISTENER \
  --ssl-policy ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-2-2021-06
```

### Performance Optimization

```bash
# Cross-zone load balancing
aws elbv2 modify-load-balancer-attributes \
  --load-balancer-arn $ALB \
  --attributes Key=load_balancing.cross_zone.enabled,Value=true

# Connection draining
aws elbv2 modify-target-group-attributes \
  --target-group-arn $TG \
  --attributes Key=deregistration_delay.timeout_seconds,Value=300

# Sticky sessions
aws elbv2 modify-target-group-attributes \
  --target-group-arn $TG \
  --attributes \
    Key=stickiness.enabled,Value=true \
    Key=stickiness.type,Value=lb_cookie \
    Key=stickiness.lb_cookie.duration_seconds,Value=86400
```

